{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"CORRECTION — my earlier comment on this ticket (\"SETTLED ... the finding is CONFIRMED\", granting bedrock:Converse) was WRONG. Superseded by runtime evidence. Recording the reversal rather than editing the record, because how I got it wrong is the useful part.\n\nWHAT I CLAIMED: that bedrock:Converse is the action to grant, on the strength of `aws iam simulate-custom-policy` reporting bedrock:Converse -> implicitDeny under an InvokeModel-only grant.\n\nWHAT IS ACTUALLY TRUE: the Converse API authorizes against **bedrock:InvokeModel**. Proven by a real call FROM the instance (SSM AWS-RunShellScript on i-00880b2c7f13527c5, as assumed-role/rebar-gerrit-instance-role) while ONLY the Converse actions were granted:\n\n  AccessDeniedException ... is not authorized to perform: bedrock:InvokeModel on resource:\n  arn:aws:bedrock:us-east-1:...:inference-profile/us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\n  because no identity-based policy allows the bedrock:InvokeModel action\n\nSo the ORIGINAL plan (grant bedrock:InvokeModel + bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream) was right, and my \"fix\" broke it. The plan text and the terraform are both corrected.\n\nWHY THE SIMULATOR MISLED ME, which is the generalizable part: bedrock:Converse genuinely EXISTS as a distinct, simulatable IAM action, so the simulator dutifully answered the question I asked — \"would this policy permit the action named bedrock:Converse?\" — and the answer was legitimately no. But that is not the question that mattered. The question that mattered was \"which action does the Converse API actually CHECK?\", and a policy simulator structurally cannot answer it. Only a real call can.\n\nThis is the SAME error class this session has been documenting, with an ironic twist: I prescribed simulation as the rigorous replacement for a live call made under bedrock:*, and then over-trusted the replacement. Both instruments were unable to observe the failure mode they were being used to rule out — the wildcard identity because it could not fail, the simulator because it answers a different question. Evidence is only as good as its ability to have come out the other way FOR THE REASON YOU CARE ABOUT.\n\nCURRENT VERIFIED STATE (terraform re-applied, 0 added / 1 changed / 0 destroyed):\n- policy rebar-gerrit-bedrock-converse on rebar-gerrit-instance-role now grants bedrock:InvokeModel + bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream, scoped to anthropic.claude-* foundation models and *.anthropic.claude-* inference profiles.\n- FROM THE INSTANCE: converse to us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6 returns \"ready\" (a real model response).\n- FROM THE INSTANCE, negative control: converse to amazon.nova-pro-v1:0 returns AccessDeniedException naming bedrock:InvokeModel on the nova ARN — so the Claude-only scope genuinely binds.\n\nVERIFICATION PROTOCOL going forward (now in the plan): simulate-principal-policy is NECESSARY but NOT SUFFICIENT; a real from-instance call is required; plus a negative control. Never validate from an operator identity holding bedrock:*."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785432070209677001,"uuid":"6405715e-82ec-4fb3-b30a-119c43a8c9c5"}